Implement frename, and the method-31 escape it arrives through

The sensor registry is not written in place. The DSP writes one entry into a
scratch file next to the registry,

    .../registry/registry/../temp.json  ->  {"sns_basic_gestures":{...}}

and then asks for that file to be moved over the real entry,

    .../registry/registry/sns_basic_gestures

It walks the whole registry that way -- around 193 entries here -- and with no
rename it writes the scratch file, fails to move it, and starts again: some
1900 rejected calls per boot, and nothing kept.

The call arrives as method 31 with three input buffers, and method 31 is not a
method. REMOTE_SCALARS_METHOD() masks the method field of `sc` with 0x1f, so it
can only name methods 0 to 30; an interface with more than that puts 31 there
and sends the real method number as the first word of the first input buffer.
Qualcomm's own generated skeleton is explicit about it:

      case 31:
      {
         uint32_t* _mid;
         if(REMOTE_SCALARS_INBUFS(_sc) < 1 || _pra[0].buf.nLen < 4) { return AEE_EBADPARM; }
         _mid = (uint32_t*)_pra[0].buf.pv;
         return _skel_invoke(*_mid, _sc, _pra);
      }

    -- qualcomm/fastrpc, src/apps_std_skel.c

apps_std has 37 methods and uses the escape for the last six. That also explains
the leading number apps_std_stat appears to take and never use: it is the method
id, 31, and stat reaches its handler the same way.

So the escape is handled where it belongs, in the listener, for any interface
with more than 32 methods; apps_std's table grows to 37 entries; and frename
goes in at 33, where it belongs. Dispatching by buffer shape would have worked
for these two and quietly mis-served ftrunc (32) or fopen_fd (34).

hexagonfs had no rename, so this adds one: a renameat op, implemented for
mapped directories only, and a hexagonfs_rename() that splits each path into
the parent directory and the name -- openat() resolves a whole path down to the
file, and renameat() wants the two halves apart.

